WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.089
Here's a question worth asking before anything leaks.

2
00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:13.559
You keep a database password in a Kubernetes Secret, the cluster's place for passwords, in the namespace called hello.

3
00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:14.572
Who can read it?

4
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:19.440
Kubernetes is the system that runs your containers on a group of machines.

5
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.856
A namespace is a named room inside it.

6
00:00:21.856 --> 00:00:25.354
And most of us would answer: only the people I allowed.

7
00:00:25.354 --> 00:00:27.389
Let's see if that's really true.

8
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:30.375
First, look inside the envelope.

9
00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:33.948
Read the Secret back, and the value comes out like this.

10
00:00:33.948 --> 00:00:35.735
Looks scrambled, doesn't it?

11
00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:36.542
It isn't.

12
00:00:36.542 --> 00:00:41.009
It's base64, which only writes the same bytes in another alphabet.

13
00:00:41.009 --> 00:00:44.935
One more command turns it straight back into the password.

14
00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:49.476
The Kubernetes docs call such a value obscured, but not secret.

15
00:00:49.476 --> 00:00:54.438
And by default the cluster's database, etcd, stores Secrets unencrypted.

16
00:00:54.438 --> 00:00:56.782
A spy novel would be disappointed.

17
00:00:56.782 --> 00:00:59.539
So the envelope itself protects nothing.

18
00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:02.463
So the obvious fix is a lock.

19
00:01:02.463 --> 00:01:09.315
Every request to Kubernetes goes through one front desk, the API server, and it checks who may do what.

20
00:01:09.315 --> 00:01:12.841
That check is called RBAC, role-based access control.

21
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.189
Here's a developer identity, dev.

22
00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:18.440
It may create Pods, the running copies of an app.

23
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.828
It has no rule about Secrets at all.

24
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.672
Ask the API server what dev may do.

25
00:01:24.672 --> 00:01:25.931
Get secrets?

26
00:01:25.931 --> 00:01:26.245
No.

27
00:01:26.245 --> 00:01:27.504
Create Pods?

28
00:01:27.504 --> 00:01:27.924
Yes.

29
00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:32.420
So dev asks for the Secret directly, and gets refused.

30
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:33.227
Forbidden.

31
00:01:33.227 --> 00:01:34.436
So far so good.

32
00:01:34.436 --> 00:01:35.645
The lock works.

33
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.243
Now back to the jam shop.

34
00:01:38.243 --> 00:01:41.462
A new helper may not open the safe in the office.

35
00:01:41.462 --> 00:01:42.251
Fair enough.

36
00:01:42.251 --> 00:01:45.011
But the helper may send the cook an order.

37
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.852
And the order says: bring me what's in the safe.

38
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.897
The front desk checks one thing: may this helper send orders to the kitchen?

39
00:01:52.897 --> 00:01:53.155
Yes.

40
00:01:53.155 --> 00:01:57.215
The cook, trusting the order, opens the safe and brings it out.

41
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.291
Nobody ever asked whether the helper was allowed in the safe.

42
00:02:01.291 --> 00:02:02.630
Each check was right.

43
00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.161
The gap is between them.

44
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:05.614
Let's map it.

45
00:02:05.614 --> 00:02:07.851
The front desk is the API server.

46
00:02:07.851 --> 00:02:12.527
The cook is the kubelet, the helper on each machine that starts Pods.

47
00:02:12.527 --> 00:02:14.424
And the order slip is a Pod.

48
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.434
dev writes a Pod called peek.

49
00:02:16.434 --> 00:02:20.608
It loads the Secret into a setting called PASSWORD, and prints it.

50
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.465
dev can't read Secrets.

51
00:02:22.465 --> 00:02:23.913
dev can create Pods.

52
00:02:23.913 --> 00:02:26.303
Will this one print the password?

53
00:02:26.303 --> 00:02:27.461
Pause and guess.

54
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:33.614
The API server checks: may dev create Pods here?

55
00:02:33.614 --> 00:02:33.949
Yes.

56
00:02:33.949 --> 00:02:35.956
pod slash peek, created.

57
00:02:36.133 --> 00:02:40.321
Then the kubelet starts it, and fetches the Secret on the Pod's behalf.

58
00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:42.101
dev reads the Pod's log.

59
00:02:42.101 --> 00:02:43.191
And there it is.

60
00:02:43.191 --> 00:02:46.119
The password the rule was meant to protect.

61
00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:46.788
Well...

62
00:02:46.788 --> 00:02:48.128
the lock was fine.

63
00:02:48.128 --> 00:02:50.809
We just ordered the cook to open it.

64
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.893
Nobody checks the creator's rights to Secrets.

65
00:02:53.893 --> 00:02:57.414
The API server only checked that dev may create the Pod.

66
00:02:57.414 --> 00:03:03.073
The same holds for anyone who may create a Deployment, the object that keeps Pods running.

67
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.609
So who can read that Secret?

68
00:03:05.609 --> 00:03:09.616
Anyone who may read Secrets, or create Pods, in its namespace.

69
00:03:09.616 --> 00:03:14.721
The namespace is the real boundary, and the Secret rule alone protects nothing.

70
00:03:15.733 --> 00:03:17.813
So protect Secrets in layers.

71
00:03:17.813 --> 00:03:25.197
One: on a real cluster, turn on encryption at rest, so etcd and its backups hold only encrypted values.

72
00:03:25.333 --> 00:03:30.624
Two: keep the list of people and programs who can create Pods in a namespace short.

73
00:03:30.624 --> 00:03:32.600
That's who holds the order pad.

74
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:40.733
Three: keep the real values in a secrets manager, one logged place that stores them, and copy them in only where needed.

75
00:03:40.733 --> 00:03:44.568
Four: never put the value in git, not even for one commit.

76
00:03:44.733 --> 00:03:47.568
And test a rule before you rely on it.

77
00:03:47.568 --> 00:03:52.939
kubectl auth can-i asks the API server directly what an identity may do.

78
00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:55.547
So the moral of the story.

79
00:03:55.547 --> 00:04:00.016
A lock on the safe means little if anyone may order the cook to open it.

80
00:04:00.016 --> 00:04:02.188
Guard the order pad, the namespace.

81
00:04:03.267 --> 00:04:04.308
A quick check.

82
00:04:04.308 --> 00:04:06.762
A Secret sits in namespace hello.

83
00:04:06.762 --> 00:04:08.845
By default, who can read it?

84
00:04:08.845 --> 00:04:10.035
Pause and think.

85
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:16.408
Anyone who may read Secrets, or create Pods, in that namespace.

86
00:04:16.408 --> 00:04:20.683
Base64 hides nothing, and the cook opens the safe for any order.
